{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{
  "experience_brief": "Desire: Owen Price, a supermarket night worker in his twenties who takes indoor bouldering far too seriously, wants to get his coworkers out when an impossible forest breaks through the shop; afterward, he wants to explore the heights he can suddenly reach. Use: Everyone receives exactly one Slot holding one skill. Owen’s alone has unlimited capacity. He must still obtain each skill and learn to use it, but combining skills lets him earn counted ranks faster than nearby single-skill users. Consequence: In the opening chapter, he chooses Cling, adds Air Step without losing it, and uses both to establish an escape route. His advancement makes the new world desirable as well as frightening. Next desire: He chooses to investigate an inhabited-looking cliff orchard for food and another skill. Coverage: Chapter 1 delivers the arrival, personal sheet, consequential skill choice, proof of his exception, rescue and response. Chapters 2–6 develop exploration, contact and a contested route home. The System’s purpose and the cause of Owen’s unique Slot remain unresolved. These events and mechanics are essential; the particular shop layout and climbing choreography are revisable."
  ,
  "world": "The System arrives everywhere on Earth at once. Its first message appears on phones, tills, televisions and directly in each person’s vision: EVERY PERSON HAS RECEIVED ONE SLOT. CHOOSE WHAT YOU WILL BE ABLE TO DO. Earth remains recognisable, but unfamiliar terrain grows through it along discontinuities: railway cuttings become ravines, lift shafts open into enormous trees, and roads emerge on the sides of forested cliffs. Ordinary life continues wherever people can keep roofs, water and food accessible. Owen’s supermarket becomes a gathering place because much of its stock survives, not because anyone designates it a settlement.\n\nEvery person receives exactly one Slot and a choice of starter skills. A skill is a specific magical permission, not a bundle of unrelated abilities. Cling allows controlled adhesion through hands and feet; Anchor holds the user stationary against external force; Kindle ignites suitable material by touch. There are no classes that confer extra skills. Mundane climbing, first aid and mechanical knowledge remain useful without occupying a Slot.\n\nLater skills occur in tangible sources, including patterned seeds from the cliff orchards. Touching an intact source reveals its skill before the person commits. Taking it consumes the source. An ordinary person must release their existing skill to install another; the abandoned skill becomes unavailable, although their memories and mundane expertise remain. Reacquiring it requires another source. Owen can take every skill he finds without releasing any. Unlimited capacity does not supply unlimited seeds, attention, stamina or safe practice.\n\nEach sheet displays Slot Rank. Installing a skill gives no advancement by itself. The System recognises demonstrated control: first successful use under a real environmental demand, materially harder applications, and genuinely new combinations. Repeating an easy manoeuvre stops earning progress, while sustained practice can prepare someone for a harder feat. Higher ranks increase the strength and endurance of installed skills without adding new magical functions. Owen has access to fresh demonstrations and combinations that nobody else can accumulate simultaneously. His advantage is structural, rather than a hidden multiplier.\n\nThe newly exposed biome consists of cliff orchards threaded by enormous roots, warm rain channels and hanging gardens. Observable traces include pruned branches, ceramic graft sleeves, sheltered sleeping hollows and handrails placed for bodies smaller than humans. Some paths have been cut deliberately. These are evidence of habitation and interrupted maintenance, not abandoned dungeon decoration.\n\nThe underlying explanation is that the orchards are cultivated territory belonging to the Vey, small, furred, six-limbed people whose communities transport pollen, maintain seasonal bridges and argue over access to graft stock. They too have single-skill Slots. Their old quarrel concerns whether distant orchards should remain connected after a root-borne blight crossed a shared bridge. Family marks on sealed paths and newer repair knots preserve that history. Earth’s arrival has broken irrigation routes and separated households. Some Vey initially mistake human buildings for a deliberate invasion; some humans mistake orchard skill-seeds for ownerless loot. Neither belief is reliable.\n\nThe Vey know how to cultivate skill-bearing plants, but they do not know who made the System. Whether Earth has moved, the orchard world has expanded, or both have been joined to something larger remains unknown. Nothing currently explains Owen’s exception."
  ,
  "opening": "Chapter 1 contains scenes 1–4, approximately 900 words each, for approximately 3,600 words total. It uses close third person through Owen and covers one connected escape, leaving room for him to react afterward.\n\nScene 1: Owen is finishing a night shift when his supervisor, Leanne, catches him revising a climbing route on his phone instead of facing shelves. His hobby has made him attentive to balance and movement, not unusually strong or fearless. He wants to finish work and reach the climbing wall before it gets crowded. The System message interrupts every active display. Messages from people elsewhere briefly establish that the event is worldwide. Then a root tears through the shop floor, tilting the loading corridor and cutting off the staff exit. Owen’s first personal sheet reads: OWEN PRICE; SLOT COUNT: 1; SLOT CAPACITY: UNLIMITED; INSTALLED SKILLS: NONE; SLOT RANK: 1. Leanne’s sheet says SLOT CAPACITY: 1. Their comparison establishes the exception without explaining it. A cleaner, Dev, is stranded beyond the widening break.\n\nScene 2: Owen considers Anchor, which could keep him safe on the shifting floor, and Kindle, which seems useful if the power stays out. He chooses Cling because he can see a route along the wall to Dev, and because the possibility of actually climbing that wall catches him despite the danger. He makes the selection with shaking hands. Cling does not move him automatically: an overloaded wrist hurts, and a bad body position still peels him away. Leanne chooses Anchor to hold a rolling stock cage while the remaining staff squeeze past. Owen uses familiar foot placement and unfamiliar adhesion to cross the damaged corridor and reach Dev. Successful control raises his Slot Rank to 2; he feels the change when his hands stop trembling under the same load. From Dev’s side, however, he discovers that the root has pulled the return wall apart. He can get back alone by a difficult detour, but Dev cannot follow him.\n\nScene 3: The exposed root carries a graft sleeve and a translucent seed. A touch identifies AIR STEP: a brief foothold on empty air, usable again after the user recovers their breath. The placement suggests cultivation, but Owen has nobody to ask. He takes it because the broken return route now has a possible solution. His sheet changes to INSTALLED SKILLS: CLING, AIR STEP; SLOT COUNT remains 1. Both abilities still work. His first attempt wastes the foothold before he transfers his weight. He rehearses the timing low over a stable ledge, where failure is recoverable, then commits to the gap. Cling lets him hold the launch position; Air Step lets him change direction across empty space; Cling catches the far wall. The successful combination advances him to Slot Rank 3. This is more than a private discovery: he has carried a line across the gap, and Leanne can anchor its safe end.\n\nScene 4: The escape takes effort after the magical breakthrough. Owen secures the line around a surviving structural upright rather than asking anyone to trust his grip indefinitely. Leanne remains fixed while Dev crosses with help from the others. Owen returns along the line instead of showing off another leap. Once everyone reaches the shop’s intact frontage, the consequences arrive: his forearms ache, Dev needs treatment, and Leanne wants to know whether taking the seed destroyed someone’s crop. Their sheets show that she remains at Rank 2 while Owen has reached Rank 3. He tells the group that he can hold more than one skill and offers to scout, but refuses an immediate climb while exhausted. Through the torn roof he can see terraces, moving lanterns and rainwater running down a built channel. The chapter ends with his changed desire: once he has rested, he wants to reach that inhabited place, both to find help and to learn what else he could become."
  ,
  "growth": "Chapter 2, scenes 5–8, approximately 3,600 words: The intact shop becomes a temporary refuge through ordinary work—treating injuries, checking food and finding usable water. Owen and Leanne investigate the orchard channel because the taps have stopped. Owen’s combined movement skills let him inspect an elevated blockage, but reaching it does not tell him how to repair it. He finds a diverted irrigation gate, fresh tool marks and a frightened Vey searching for a missing relative. The chapter changes his objective from collecting useful things to making contact with someone whose home has also been damaged. Leanne’s repeated, increasingly demanding use of Anchor establishes that single-skill people can become formidable specialists.\n\nChapter 3, scenes 9–12, approximately 3,600 words: Contact develops through shared work and uncertain gestures rather than instant translation. The Vey, Serri, needs access to a stranded nursery before its grafts dry out. Owen needs permission to draw water without ruining someone else’s supply. Each can reach places the other cannot safely manage. Repairing the channel earns a limited water-sharing agreement, and Serri offers a cultivated Threadline seed in return for help reaching the nursery. Threadline produces a temporary tensile cord between touched surfaces. Owen wants it because carrying rope nearly defeated the first rescue. He installs it, but initially makes lines that slacken when his concentration shifts. Taking the skill is immediate; making it dependable is a chapter-level problem.\n\nChapter 4, scenes 13–16, approximately 3,600 words: The nursery expedition requires Owen to combine adhesion, air footholds and temporary lines while Serri identifies safe grafts and dangerous roots. The resulting advancement carries him ahead of his companions, but a route that suits him proves unsuitable for people carrying water and children. A failed line drops supplies onto a lower terrace and costs the group a long retrieval. Nobody loses earned ranks or acquired skills. Owen instead loses daylight and confidence, and must help build a slower, partly mundane route. His private pursuit of beautiful movement begins to become a desire to make extraordinary places accessible to other people.\n\nChapter 5, scenes 17–20, approximately 3,600 words: The repaired route reaches a sealed orchard boundary. Serri’s relatives want it reopened to reunite households; another Vey community fears renewed blight. Human scavengers have also begun removing skill-seeds without understanding the grafts. Owen’s mobility makes him useful to every side without making him the rightful decision-maker. He investigates the cut bridges with a local cultivator and discovers an active diseased root beyond the seal. Access to further skills now depends on whether he spends his time helping protect the orchard or simply takes what he can carry. He chooses the slower cooperative route because its people and continued cultivation matter to the future he wants.\n\nChapter 6, scenes 21–24, approximately 3,600 words: A storm drives runoff through the damaged boundary toward both the nursery and the shop. Owen uses his broader skill set to move between isolated work parties, while Leanne’s stronger Anchor and Vey specialist skills handle loads he cannot. The resolution is a working diversion and a traversable evacuation route, not victory over the whole apocalypse. Owen’s counted advancement continues to outpace the people around him because each new skill opens further meaningful combinations. His next desired capability is Load Share, a grafted skill that could spread weight across his temporary lines; he has seen its source in the nursery and understands why its cultivators will not surrender it casually.\n\nBeyond this opening arc, greater rank could let Owen cling through violent weather, sustain longer crossings and build temporary routes through moving terrain. New skills would expand what he can attempt, but he must still choose where to go, whom to help and what to practise. Injury, failed expeditions and lost equipment can delay him without confiscating his defining advantage. His accumulated skills, knowledge of routes and relationships survive those setbacks. The larger promise is not merely that he can own every power: it is that he can eventually explore—and bring others into—places no single-skill person could cross alone."
}"}}
